**A single-shot timer that fires once after N milliseconds.**

<!-- ELABORATION: one or two sentences that flesh out the tagline.  Name the
     concrete user value and flag any non-obvious constraint a cold reader
     would want to know inside ~10 seconds.  Be honest about caveats, and
     soften sweeping claims that aren't bench-verified. -->

Set a deadline, poll each tick, and get `True` once when time's up.  No `time.sleep()`, no callbacks.

{family_note}## Install

{install_block}## Quick example

<!-- QUICK EXAMPLE: 5-12 lines.  Must actually run without modification on
     at least one runtime.  Show the most common use case, not the most
     advanced.  Add an inline comment showing the expected output so readers
     can verify their setup works.  If the package is network-shaped (needs
     wifi credentials), use placeholders ("your-network" / "your-password")
     so the example doesn't look like a real working endpoint. -->

```python
from {import_name} import Countdown
from chumicro_timing import ticks_ms

countdown = Countdown(period_ms=5000)    # fires once after 5 s

while True:
    now = ticks_ms()
    if countdown.fired(now):
        print("time's up")               # prints "time's up" after ~5 seconds
        break
```

## What's included

<!-- API SUMMARY: tables grouped by purpose.  One table per major feature
     group, not one giant catch-all.  Two shapes are in use across the
     published packages: a small surface splits into two or three groups
